---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { EMAIL, GITHUB_URL } from '../consts';
import { decisions } from '../data/decisions';
import { experiments } from '../data/lab';

const answered = experiments.filter((e) => e.state === 'answered').length;
const open = experiments.filter((e) => e.state === 'open').length;
---

<Base
  title="Contact"
  description="Software architect who builds AI systems. Ten years across domains where being wrong has consequences."
>
  <div class="portrait-row">
    <img src="/portrait.jpg" alt="Serhiy Kucherenko" width="64" height="64" loading="lazy" />
    <div>
      <h1 style="margin:0;font-size:1.3rem;">Contact</h1>
      <div class="meta">remote · freelance · <span style="color:var(--ok)">● open</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="claim">
    <h2>Software architect who builds AI systems</h2>
    <p>
      Ten years of production .NET across banking, medical, industrial, travel and invoicing,
      and internal tooling. Regulated and not. Two-person teams and large orgs.
    </p>
    <p>
      The through-line is domains where being wrong has consequences: a wrong payment, a wrong
      medical record, a wrong instruction on a factory floor. That instinct is what I now point
      at AI systems. A retriever that refuses to answer rather than guess without a grounded
      source is the same instinct, wearing different clothes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p class="eyebrow">the evidence, at three altitudes</p>
  <div class="pcards">
    <div class="pcard">
      <span class="pnum">{decisions.length} decision records</span>
      <p>
        Each one names the alternative that lost and the price of the one that won. Including
        the prediction that measurement proved wrong.
      </p>
      <a href="/decisions/">read the decisions →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="pcard">
      <span class="pnum">2 systems running</span>
      <p>
        A retriever that cites the page it answered from, and this site. Both live, both
        documented down to the trade-offs.
      </p>
      <a href="/systems/">see how they are built →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="pcard">
      <span class="pnum">{answered} answers, {open} open</span>
      <p>
        Techniques measured and then declined on the evidence. A "no" is a finding, and it is
        recorded like one.
      </p>
      <a href="/lab/">see what was tested →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="edges">
    <p class="eyebrow">where the edges are</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>I build systems around models. I do not train them.</li>
      <li>AI experience is measured in weeks. The judgment it rests on is measured in years.</li>
      <li>Large-scale distributed AI infrastructure is not something I have demonstrated.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div class="cta">
    <div class="ask">
      <div class="l1">Write me a paragraph about the problem.</div>
      <div class="l2">If I can't help, I'll say so.</div>
    </div>
    <a class="btn" href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>✉ {EMAIL}</a>
  </div>
  <p class="meta">
    <a href={GITHUB_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the code speaks too ↗</a>
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .claim {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;
  }
  .claim h2 {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.7rem;
  }
  .claim p {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 0 0 0.7rem;
  }
  .pnum {
    display: block;
    color: var(--accent);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }
  .edges {
    border: 1px dashed var(--line);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 1rem 1.15rem;
    margin: 1.75rem 0;
  }
  .edges .eyebrow {
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  .edges ul {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--dim);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
  .edges li {
    margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
  }
</style>
